#speculation(title: [Fibrinogen Chemistry, Not Aggregate Count, Carries the Microclot Signal])[
*Certainty: 0.35.* (Reason: the count-versus-chemistry distinction is directly observed, but the fibrinogen modification signal sits in the Long COVID subgroups rather than in the pre-COVID group that best matches ME/CFS.) The null result and the positive reports can both be correct if they measured different properties of the same material. A well-controlled cohort counted fibrin(ogen) microaggregates and found no excess @Fraser2026LCcoagulation. A proteomic study of the insoluble plasma fraction found few proteins significantly dysregulated but extensive disease-specific post-translational modifications, with the fibrinogen signal concentrated in the Long COVID groups @Booyens2026POTSPTM. Counting particles and profiling their chemistry are not the same measurement.

The hypothesis is that the informative signal is chemical rather than numerical: fibrinogen in post-infectious plasma carries a modification pattern that alters clot behaviour without increasing the number or total area of aggregates. This is the narrowest version of the microclot claim that survives the abundance null, and it is narrower in a way that matters — it does not require the particles to be more numerous, only differently built.

*Falsifiable prediction:* on one plasma panel where aggregate count and area are equal between patients and controls, the fibrinogen post-translational-modification profile should still separate the groups, and the modification burden should correlate with clot lysis time even where aggregate count does not. Equal modification profiles, or a modification signal explained by individual aggregate burden rather than by group membership, refutes the hypothesis.

*Not yet tested in ME/CFS, and not yet supported by the closest available group.* The modification data come from pre-COVID postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome and Long COVID groups (18 controls, 20 pre-COVID POTS, 19 Long COVID with orthostatic intolerance, 18 Long COVID with POTS), not from an ME/CFS cohort @Booyens2026POTSPTM. The distribution matters more than the group count: the fibrinogen modification signal was concentrated in Long COVID with initial orthostatic hypotension, while the pre-COVID POTS group — the one closest to a non-COVID post-infectious illness — showed fewer fibrinogen modifications @Booyens2026POTSPTM. A second caveat is pre-analytical: the samples underwent two freeze-thaw cycles, which can itself generate post-translational modifications, so part of any modification signal may be handling artefact @Booyens2026POTSPTM. The ME/CFS arm of this hypothesis is therefore an inference from the weakest-supported direction of the source data, not a finding.

*Consequence:* If confirmed, the useful test becomes a fibrinogen chemistry panel rather than a particle count, and the therapeutic target becomes fibrinogen modification rather than clot removal. That is a different research programme from the one the field has been running. (Origin: brainstorm.)
] <spec:microclot-composition-not-abundance>
